<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    NuGet merges configuration from the drive root downward, plus the user- and machine-level files, so
    without a config here a restore inherits whatever sources a given machine happens to have. <clear /> is
    what makes this repository authoritative: it discards every source defined above this file, so a fresh
    clone and a CI runner restore from exactly the one source below and nothing else.

    Package source mapping is the second half, and its value here is not defence against a second source
    (there is only one) but keeping that true. With a mapping in place an ADDED source serves nothing until
    it is given its own pattern, so a new feed cannot quietly start answering for package ids that resolve
    to nuget.org today. The surprising half is worth stating plainly: adding a source therefore means adding
    a pattern for it here, or its packages will never be found.

    Central Package Management is why this matters more than it otherwise would. NU1507 is raised as soon as
    more than one source is configured under CPM, and its documented fix is this mapping or a single source,
    so the alternative to declaring it is a restore that breaks the day someone adds a feed.

    Not declared here: audit sources. NuGetAudit already reaches nuget.org for advisory data through the
    source below, and on net10 it audits the whole dependency graph by default.

    The file name is lower-case on purpose. Path lookup is case-sensitive on Linux and macOS, where this
    repository also builds, and `nuget.config` is the spelling NuGet looks for there.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>

  <packageSourceMapping>
    <clear />
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
